        'Utility waste hides because nobody owns it. Treat it like any waste: go and see. The weekend test shows the base load: air compressors still loading (leaks, often 20 to 30% of output in plants without a leak programme; see Motors, drives and fluid power), steam still flowing, chillers and tower fans running, pumps circulating for nothing.\n\nWrite each finding down with an estimate of kW and hours.',
        'Savings need an energy baseline: the energy a plant would have used without the change, normalised for output. A common form is a straight line fitted through monthly data: energy = fixed part + variable part × tonnes.\n\nThe fixed part is the base load, the variable part the kWh per tonne. A drop in the fixed part after a leak campaign is visible; a drop hidden by a busier month is not.',
        'Prioritise by money and effort, and in the right order: first reduce demand (switch off, fix leaks, lower pressures and temperatures), then make the supply efficient, then change the source.\n\nPayback time, investment divided by yearly cash saving, is the common first filter; Cost engineering covers NPV and the rest. Fix leaks before sizing a new compressor or boiler, or you buy capacity for losses.',
        'In the Netherlands the energy-saving obligation applies to sites using 50,000 kWh of electricity or 25,000 m³ of natural gas (or equivalent) a year or more. They must take all measures with a payback time of five years or less, calculated with a legally fixed method and prices.\n\nThey report every four years, and RVO’s recognised measures list names accepted measures per sector. Very large users have a separate audit duty.',
        'Experts know savings fade: a fixed leak returns, a valve gets closed again. Savings stick with an owner, a daily or weekly kWh per tonne on the board, and a check in the routine.\n\nAvoid double counting between projects that claim the same kWh. A sharp question: “Who looks at this utility’s number every week, and what do they do when it rises?”',
